{#
  BASE LAYOUT
  -----------
  This is the shared page "shell" that every migrated page uses — it's
  the Eleventy/Nunjucks equivalent of what used to be copy-pasted at the
  top and bottom of every hand-written HTML file (the <head>, navbar,
  footer, and closing scripts).

  HOW A PAGE USES THIS FILE (see src/pages/home.njk for the real example):
    {% extends "layouts/base.njk" %}
    {% block pageStyle %} ... page-specific CSS ... {% endblock %}
    {% block content %} ... page-specific HTML ... {% endblock %}
    {% block pageScript %} ... page-specific JS ... {% endblock %}

  {% extends %} means "start from this other template, then let me fill
  in specific sections." Each {% block name %}...{% endblock %} below is
  a named slot. A page that extends this layout can define its own
  {% block name %} with the SAME name, and Nunjucks swaps that block's
  content into this exact spot. If a page doesn't define a block, the
  layout's own (usually empty) version of that block is used instead.

  This is different from a plain HTML comment (angle-bracket-bang-dash
  style), which still gets sent to the browser. A Nunjucks-style comment
  like this whole block is stripped out during the build — it never
  appears in the page source at all. Good for notes-to-future-editors;
  use an HTML comment instead when you want a marker that survives into
  the rendered HTML (like the "HERO" style comments already used
  throughout this site).

  locale.pathPrefix (Phase 2, Year Archives): every page used to live
  flat at the site root, so a relative link like "assets/common.css"
  always worked. Year Archive pages are the first ones nested one
  level down (src/pages/years.njk → /years/2025.html), where that same
  relative link would resolve to the wrong place (/years/assets/...).
  pathPrefix lets a page's own locale object say how many directories
  "up" to go to reach the site root — "" for every existing root-level
  page (the default below, so none of them needed to change), "../"
  for pages under years/. Used here and in navbar.njk wherever a link
  points at another page or a static asset (not needed for external
  URLs, which are already absolute).
#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ locale.lang }}" dir="{{ locale.dir }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>{{ locale.pageTitle }}</title>

  <!-- Google Fonts -->
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Cairo:wght@300;400;600;700&family=Poppins:wght@300;400;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">

  {#
    The original site loaded a different Bootstrap CSS build depending
    on language: bootstrap.rtl.min.css for Arabic pages (RTL = right to
    left, so it mirrors margins, text-align, etc.) and the regular
    bootstrap.min.css for English. We reproduce that here by branching
    on locale.dir, which home.js already set to "rtl" or "ltr".
  #}
  {% if locale.dir == "rtl" %}
  <!-- Bootstrap RTL + Icons -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/css/bootstrap.rtl.min.css">
  {% else %}
  <!-- Bootstrap 5 + Icons -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css">
  {% endif %}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-icons@1.11.3/font/bootstrap-icons.css">

  <!-- Shared Styles (unchanged from the original site) -->
  <link href="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}assets/common.css" rel="stylesheet">

  {#
    Site search (Phase 3): Pagefind's default UI ships its own stylesheet
    alongside the search index it builds (see package.json's "build"
    script and .github/workflows/deploy.yml — both run
    `npx pagefind --site _site` after Eleventy renders the pages, which
    writes _site/pagefind/pagefind-ui.css and .js). Loaded on every page
    since the search trigger lives in the shared navbar. common.css's
    --pagefind-ui-* overrides below theme it to match the brand colors
    instead of Pagefind's default purple.
  #}
  <link href="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}pagefind/pagefind-ui.css" rel="stylesheet">

  <style>
  {% if locale.dir == "rtl" %}
  {# This body font-order + btn-lang spacing rule only ever appeared in
     the Arabic page's own <style> block in the original site — English
     pages didn't have it. Keeping it here, guarded by the same "rtl"
     check, reproduces that exactly. #}
  /* RTL-specific adjustments */
  body{
  font-family: Cairo, Poppins, system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI, Roboto, Arial, sans-serif;
  }
  .btn-lang{
  margin-inline-start:1.25rem;
  }
  {% endif %}

  {% block pageStyle %}{% endblock %}
  </style>
</head>

{#
  locale.bodyClass is optional, per-page (set in a page's own
  src/_data/pages/*.js, not in site.*.json, since it's page-specific
  layout, not shared chrome) — most pages don't set it and get a plain
  <body>. Submissions is the first page that needs it: its original
  markup set class="bg-light text-dark" on <body>, which (thanks to
  Bootstrap's !important on utility classes) actually overrides
  common.css's usual body background for that one page.
#}
{#
  data-pagefind-body (Phase 3): once this attribute appears anywhere on
  the site, Pagefind restricts indexing everywhere to just the elements
  inside a tagged region, and skips entirely any page that has no such
  region. Putting it on <body> means every Eleventy-rendered page is
  indexed in full automatically — no per-page template changes needed —
  while admin/index.html (the separate, hand-written Decap CMS entry
  point; not rendered through this layout, has no such attribute) is
  excluded for free, same as any future non-Eleventy static file would
  be. navbar.njk/footer.njk carry data-pagefind-ignore so the same nav
  labels and footer boilerplate aren't indexed as "content" on every
  single page's search result.
#}
<body{% if locale.bodyClass %} class="{{ locale.bodyClass }}"{% endif %} data-pagefind-body>
  {% include "partials/navbar.njk" %}

  {% block content %}{% endblock %}

  {% include "partials/footer.njk" %}

  {#
    Search modal (Phase 3): one shared instance per page, opened by the
    search-icon button in navbar.njk's assoc-fixed area. Lives here
    rather than inside navbar.njk itself since it's page-level chrome
    (a modal must be a direct child in the DOM, not nested inside the
    <nav> it's triggered from) — same reasoning as navbar/footer being
    separate includes rather than one giant partial.
  #}
  <div class="modal fade" id="searchModal" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="searchModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-scrollable modal-lg modal-dialog-centered">
      <div class="modal-content">
        <div class="modal-header">
          <h5 class="modal-title" id="searchModalLabel">{{ locale.site.search.modalTitle }}</h5>
          <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="modal" aria-label="{{ locale.site.search.modalCloseAriaLabel }}"></button>
        </div>
        <div class="modal-body">
          <div id="pagefind-search"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Bootstrap JS -->
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js"></script>
  <script src="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}assets/common.js"></script>

  <!-- Pagefind UI bundle (generated at build time, see package.json) -->
  <script src="{{ locale.pathPrefix | default('') }}pagefind/pagefind-ui.js"></script>
  <script>
  {#
    Initialized lazily on the modal's own "show" event rather than at
    page load — PagefindUI() itself is cheap, but there's no reason to
    build the search box before anyone has asked to search. Guarded
    with a flag since show.bs.modal fires every time the modal opens,
    not just the first time.
  #}
  (function () {
    var modalEl = document.getElementById("searchModal");
    var initialized = false;
    modalEl.addEventListener("show.bs.modal", function () {
      if (initialized) return;
      initialized = true;
      new PagefindUI({
        element: "#pagefind-search",
        showSubResults: true,
        showImages: false,
        translations: {{ locale.site.search.translations | dump | safe }}
      });
    });
    modalEl.addEventListener("shown.bs.modal", function () {
      var input = modalEl.querySelector(".pagefind-ui__search-input");
      if (input) input.focus();
    });
  })();
  </script>

  <script>
  {% block pageScript %}{% endblock %}
  </script>
</body>
</html>
